// DMS session spawn. Unlike Azir (which keeps GNOME and deliberately does NOT
// --global enable DMS as a systemd unit, spawning it from niri instead so it
// can't leak into the GNOME session), Roshar follows the exact same pattern —
// this is the single line that makes "DMS runs as the niri session's shell"
// true at all. Without it, niri would come up with no bar, no launcher.
spawn-at-startup "dms" "run"
